body:
@rocallahan, @dherman, @BrendanEich: couldn't we produce a <script cors src=""> attribute by having:

1. The <a script cors src=""> attribute tells the browser to do CORS to fetch the script
2. The server refuses to serve the script to any browser which doesn't provide the attribute (this is obviously a pain on the server, but not impossible)

@ekr: exactly what I was thinking of, yes.

@rocallahan: if people depend on it, we can't remove it. If they don't, we can't anyway because it's hard to be sure, and any browser who goes "first" risks losing share even at the margins, so it's rare to find anyone who'll go first. In practice it's hard to remove stuff unless it was a steaming flop from a previous rapid release cycle.

I don't see why, given ongoing obligate status of <script src=>, we wouldn't separate concerns and not make type=module mandate CORS. Unless there is some hope to up-sell CORS via module (both are frail enough that looks like a down-sell to bare script from all I can see), or vice versa.

Even if most uses of <script type=module src=...> want CORS because most are in Intranet or public but auth'ed gmail-style web apps, some script tags will want to source from edge-cached universal modules. I suspect most by dynamic and even static frequency. So "do both". They're independent attributes.

A vain hope of eliminating <script src=> should not enter into the design, especially if we haven't validated any of the current coupling of type=module and CORS against the real world yet!

@ekr thanks for chiming in. It's not about some script, it's about **some sensitive resource** _A_ that contains secrets/information and either is or evaluates (again I mean what I write) like a module script. The attacker cannot get to _A_ directly since it doesn't use CORS and uses either credentials or a firewall to hide its information.

If you do `<script src={A}>` the information doesn't come out (you might get a masked exception). If you do `<script module src={A}>` the information does come out. (Through the requests that are exposed to a service worker or the Loader API, or something new we haven't thought of yet.)


@BrendanEich you follow "I shall strive to do better." with typical Twitter bullying tactics. I'm happy to talk with you, but lets organize a Hangouts or something that is a little less toxic. Again, what I want is to get a common understanding on the issues and then explore solutions. Despite sayings to the contrary, I haven't really been advocating CORS, I'm just trying to explain the issue at hand as I see it. There is a complex set of tradeoffs, the Chrome team picks CORS since that is guaranteed to be safe, but there are other options, that have their own drawbacks and perhaps advantages in ease of deployment. But it's hard to discuss those if we're not all on the same page.

I also think that @domenic (on behalf of Google it seems like) and @rocallahan are making a slightly different argument from me. They would like CORS "just because", since it removes all risk. As I mentioned to @dherman at a Mozilla event in December I think I would personally be okay with requiring a JavaScript MIME type. That does have the problem that @rocallahan points out, that you need to be very careful with secrets, but you already need to be that with JavaScript in general. By requiring a JavaScript MIME type, it's at least reduced to those resources, not everything that might evaluate as JavaScript modules.

@annevk: thanks. I have some followup questions.

- Do we actually know of a non-artificial resource that has this structure? "import foo" isn't valid JS outside of modules, right?
- Suppose that instead A consisted of ```<script src="X">``` we would have the same problem, right?

@ekr: right, Script cannot produce `import` sentences in JS.

But push in the other direction on the degree of freedom at hand -- the option to have SW+loader specs keep non-CORS (under the assumption this issue is resolved to remove the CORS mandate for script type=module) cross-origin requests via imports from leaking, just as the SW/HTML spec does for non-CORS cross-origin requests of other kinds.

Why would we assume that the import-induced information leak must be permitted in the no-CORS alternative, when we intentionally spec to preclude equivalent-under-transpiling leaks from old-style script src= world? Or if we don't preclude, then what's the new threat? Anyway, it seems fair to raise since the opaque response idea was already used here at an earlier stage of the discussion.

@annevk: You're right, you didn't call me ignorant, only my pal @domenic did (but then you schooled him on opaque handles being responses not requests, lol). i still think you misread my tweet, as noted on Twitter, but I'm sorry if it came off as attacking you (which would be silly, since it was you who schooled us on the opaque handle business). I don't care about who forgot what subtle corner case; that's a sideshow and a distraction.

(In other news, David was mean to Goliath. That's one version of the story, anyway! ;-)

I'm still non-combatively interested in your thoughts on censoring non-CORS cross-origin script type=module import leaks, under the assumption CORS is not required. It could be that this is too much of a hack, I get that -- but it addresses the threat you raised, unless I'm missing something.

@domenic It's frustrating to hear you claim that this was a collaborative decision but to have so many of my points consistently go unanswered, or get met with nothing more than "we're not convinced." It feels like the only way I can get attention to this issue is to get some other people involved. :(

@annevk I'm having trouble understanding this part:

> I think my main problem with the alternative...is that I suspect we'll forget about this hole down the road. And the reason I think that is because this is something we would have to add specifically for `<script type=module>`. The bypassing of the service worker for subresources.

Can you decode that for me?
